          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus identifies the Sabbath day chiefly with sacred rest—creation rest, present rest in Christ, and final eschatological rest—while also recognizing it as a covenantal and calendrical observance.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies yom ha-shabbat structurally as the marked day or member of a Sabbath unit, analogous to the new-moon day within a month. It distinguishes this from the resting member itself, which it associates with the doubled expression shabbat shabbaton.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The Sabbath day is fundamentally a sacred day of rest that typologically signifies spiritual and eschatological rest.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The Sabbath day is the marked calendrical member of a Sabbath unit, not lexically the rest itself.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus applies the term’s central symbolism to creation rest, rest in Christ, and final rest. The book instead confines the phrase’s direct identification to a marked day within a calendrical unit and reallocates the explicit resting-member sense to shabbat shabbaton, including its occurrence on the seventh day, the Day of Atonement, or a sabbatical year.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"As primary identifications, sacred or typological rest and the marked member of a Sabbath unit are different referents. The book expressly distinguishes the day-expression from the resting member, replacing the consensus’s rest identification at the lexical-symbolic level. This does not by itself exclude the possibility that the day could secondarily symbolize rest.\",\"rationale\":\"This is more than added precision because the book’s controlling identification is not merely a narrower species of sacred rest; it is a different structural referent, the marked member of a Sabbath unit. Its explicit contrast with shabbat shabbaton requires reclassifying the rest identification central to the consensus. Nevertheless, the disagreement may partly reflect different analytical levels: the consensus offers theological typology, whereas the entry emphasizes lexical structure.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The strongest objection is that the two claims can coexist: a calendrical day can be the marked member of a Sabbath unit and also symbolize rest in Christ. Because the book does not explicitly deny secondary typology, its account could be viewed as a novel lexical analysis rather than a contradiction. The divergent classification rests on its explicit denial that the day-expression itself identifies the resting member.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
        }
